# Mock test (HackerRank screening) structure

A mock test is a **module inside a course** whose id ends with `-mock`. It simulates a HackerRank screening: timed, three fixed sections, scored quiz + coding.

**HackerRank alignment:** matches common screening **shape** (instructions → MCQ → stdin/stdout coding) and **grading style** (exact stdout per test case). It is **not** a full platform clone — see [`.cursor/skills/create-mock-test/reference.md`](../../.cursor/skills/create-mock-test/reference.md#hackerrank-alignment) for what aligns, what differs, and official HackerRank doc links.

## Activity counts (fixed)

| Section | Count | Scored activities | Content |
|---------|-------|-------------------|---------|
| 1. Instructions | **1** section | **0** | `README.md` only — rules, duration, constraints |
| 2. Multiple choice | **1** quiz | **4–10** questions | `quiz/quiz.json` (one file) |
| 3. Coding challenge | **1** project | **≥1** `tests.json` case with `expectedStdout` and/or `expectedExitCode` | `projects/001-<slug>/starter/` |

**Totals per mock test:** exactly **3 sections**, **1 quiz**, **1 coding project**, **0** extra section types.

Do not add a fourth section, reorder sections, or split coding into multiple projects without updating `mock-test.meta.json` and frontend.

## Folder layout

```text
course/<course>/modules/<NN>-<topic>-mock/
  module.meta.json
  mock-test.meta.json          # duration, passingScorePercent, sections[]
  README.md
  lessons/
    01.1-test-instructions/    # mockTestSection: instructions
    01.2-multiple-choice/      # mockTestSection: quiz → quiz/quiz.json
    01.3-coding-challenge/     # mockTestSection: coding → projects/001-<slug>/
```

Module id **must** end with `-mock`. Catalog emits these as `course.mockTests[]` (separate from `course.modules[]`).

## Scope

- **Topic boundary:** assess the **parent study module** linked by `graphIndex` in `module.meta.json` (e.g. `01` → JavaScript Fundamentals). Pull questions and coding tasks from that module's lessons — do not invent topics outside the graph.
- **Instructions section:** state what is assessed, time limit, pass threshold, section order, Node.js-only constraint.
- **Quiz section:** conceptual knowledge — types, operators, control flow, APIs covered in the source module. Use `create-course-quiz` schema.
- **Coding section:** one stdin/stdout problem scoped to module fundamentals. Every **functional requirement** in the project README must map to a scored `tests.json` case.
- **Out of scope for `tests.json`:** naming conventions, immutability, or other rules not verifiable via stdin/stdout — document in README only.

## Evaluation

### Metadata (`mock-test.meta.json`)

```json
{
  "durationMinutes": 90,
  "passingScorePercent": 70,
  "sections": [
    { "lessonId": "01.1-test-instructions", "type": "instructions" },
    { "lessonId": "01.2-multiple-choice", "type": "quiz" },
    { "lessonId": "01.3-coding-challenge", "type": "coding" }
  ]
}
```

- `durationMinutes` — timed session length shown in UI (HackerRank duration is recruiter-configured; often 30–90+ min).
- `passingScorePercent` — pass threshold shown in UI (e.g. 70%). Real HackerRank often uses percentile benchmarks, not a fixed % — [benchmarking](https://www.hackerrank.com/writing/how-hackerrank-benchmarks-passing-scores-for-senior-engineer-data-structures-tests).
- `sections[]` — must match lesson folder ids and each lesson's `mockTestSection`.

### Per-section scoring

| Section | How it is evaluated |
|---------|---------------------|
| Instructions | Not scored — orientation only |
| Quiz | Each question: select option → **Check answer** → correct/incorrect + explanation. One point per question (equal weight). HackerRank may use multi-select and negative marking — [MCQ scoring](https://support.hackerrank.com/articles/2513748038-multiple-choice-questions). |
| Coding | **Run answer** executes all `tests.json` cases. Cases with `expectedStdout` or `expectedExitCode` → **Pass/Fail**. Cases without those fields → smoke **Ran** only (not scored). HackerRank also uses hidden cases and separate Run vs Submit — [during-test FAQ](https://candidatesupport.hackerrank.com/articles/2859650598-frequently-asked-questions-during-the-test). |

### Coding `tests.json` contract

- `stdin` piped to `process.stdin`; compare `expectedStdout` with `trimEnd`.
- Include an `example` case matching the README I/O plus edge cases (zeros, negatives, bounds) as separate cases.
- At least one scored case required so Run answer shows Pass/Fail.

## Authoring checklist

1. Scaffold with `create-mock-test` skill; add `.cursor-created.json` to every new folder.
2. Module README: overview, duration, passing score, section order, no-refresh note.
3. Validate: `validate-quiz.mjs` + `validate-project.mjs`.
4. Regenerate: `cd frontend && npm run catalog:generate`.
5. Spot-check: `/course/<courseId>/module/<moduleId>/mock-test`.

